<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chūgoku region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chugoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chūgoku region is the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, made up of the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, Shimane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and Yamaguchi. Its mountains divide a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast from the Inland Sea coast. In the 16th century the Mōri clan conquered most of the region, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> it was divided among domains such as Chōshū, Hiroshima, Okayama and Tottori.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chugoku-region.jpg" alt="Chūgoku region" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chugoku_Region_in_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Chūgoku region, also called the San'in-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanyo-region">San'yō region</a>, is the westernmost region of Honshū. It consists of the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, Yamaguchi, Shimane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, and neighbours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and Kansai. The land is characterised by irregular rolling hills and limited plains, and mountains running east and west divide it in two. San'in, the yin of the mountains, is the northern part facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, and San'yō, the yang of the mountains, the southern part facing the Seto Inland Sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chūgoku literally means middle country, but the origin of the name is unclear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was divided into provinces called koku, classified by distance from the administrative centre in Kansai into near, middle and far countries. One explanation is that Chūgoku first referred to the middle countries west of the capital, but only five of the provinces normally counted in the region were so classified. An alternative is that it meant the provinces between Kansai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, historically the link between Japan and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Historically the name covered the 16 provinces of the San'indō and San'yōdō, though some of the easternmost were later absorbed into prefectures based in Kansai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, regarded as the capital of the region, was rebuilt after being destroyed by an atomic bomb in 1945 and is now an industrial city of more than one million people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> is the other large centre. The San'yō coast is a zone of heavy industry, while the San'in is less industrialised, with an agricultural economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chūgoku region” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The domain covered some ten provinces in the <b>Chugoku</b> region and had a powerful navy.</blockquote><a href="/state/mori-clan-domain">Mori clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Its economy rested on rice, cotton and iron sand smelting in the <b>Chugoku</b> mountains, and it guarded the Sea of Japan coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/tottori-domain">Tottori Domain</a></li></ul></section>
